Rule of thumb: Tr=5 is the same structure with the two outermost rings removed — not a truncated copy of Tr=7 code.

0. Ground rules
Do not copy an existing Tr=5 implementation. Derive it.
Analyse first, output a plan, get confirmation, then edit files.
Never overwrite the input file. Write to a new file.
Keep the shared routing tail in exactly one place.
1. Derive the ring model (analysis only, no edits)
Let P = Pw + Ps. For every geometry block, express its Y offset as k * P.

Output a table:

k	role	layer	structure	mirror
0	outer end	M4	single grad	MY
1,3,5	M3 middle	M3	double grad	R180
2,4	M4 middle	M4	double grad	R180
6	inner end	M4	single grad	MY
Confirm three facts from the code itself:

k runs 0..6 → number of rings equals Tr.
Even k → M4, odd k → M3.
k=6 is the fixed anchor — the inner end always uses (Pw+Ps)*6, in every turn-count variant. The outer end moves instead.
General form:

代码块


kStart = 7 - Tr
Tr=7 -> k = 0..6      (4x M4, 3x M3)
Tr=5 -> k = 2..6      (3x M4, 2x M3)
2. Derive the Tr=5 node table
k	role	near side	mirror side
2	outer end (re-generated!)	out0	out0m
3	M3 middle	m3pts[0]	m3mpts[0]
4	M4 middle	m4pts[0]	m4mpts[0]
5	M3 middle	m3pts[1]	m3mpts[1]
6	inner end (unchanged)	in6	in6m
Consequences:

m4pts length = 1, m3pts length = 2.
k=2 must be re-drawn as the outer-end structure (single-side grad + MY). It is not a normal middle ring any more.
k=6 stays (Pw+Ps)*6. Never rename it or shrink it.
Loop mapping (pick one, state which):

